-- Coherence accept (FQN dispatch — constructor module-qualified identity):
-- two DISTINCT same-short-name types (`NA.Thing`/`NB.Thing`, nested modules)
-- each implement the SAME general interface `Speak` AND each declare a
-- constructor named `Shared` — a "double collision": same short type name AND a
-- shared ctor name. This shape was rejected under the interim Task-6b stopgap:
-- the constructor-tag identity the backends and interpreter route dispatch on
-- used to key on the BARE ctor name (`ci_module` was diagnostic-only, feeding no
-- dispatch), so `speak` would silently MISDISPATCH (registration-order-dependent;
-- interpreted and compiled could disagree). The constructor module-qualified
-- identity plan resolves ctor identity upstream — native ECon/pattern-match
-- qualify a colliding type's ctor key with its declaring module, and the
-- interpreter qualifies the VCon tag the same way — so `speak` dispatches
-- correctly on the value's real type in BOTH backends. The declaring-module
-- coherence relaxation is therefore sound unconditionally; no ctor-sharing
-- carve-out remains. Accept twin with DISTINCT ctor names: accept/t89.
-- Cross-backend runtime witness: test/imports/speak_double_collision_native.
-- Design: specs/plans/2026-07-20-fqn-impl-dispatch-identity.md.
mod Top do
  interface Speak(a) do
    fn speak : a -> String
  end
  mod NA do
    type Thing = Shared | OnlyA
    impl Speak(Thing) do
      fn speak(_self) do "from-A" end
    end
  end
  mod NB do
    type Thing = Shared | OnlyB
    impl Speak(Thing) do
      fn speak(_self) do "from-B" end
    end
  end
  fn main() do 0 end
end
